After a while, one does anyway; my PCs specialize in
dying a lot, so I've already contributed about 0.5%
of the Hearse database for my (MS-Windows) platform,
sooner or later I'll start getting some of my own
bones back, which is perfectly okay, that happens by
sheer luck once in a while on the big multiplayer
NetHack hosts Hearse trys to emulate, too.

But I think the behavior of Hearse is more complex
than you indicate. Hearse does not, for example,
check _every_ bones file against the Hearse
database. If the Hearse executable sees nothing new
in the way of a bones file, it doesn't bother
contacting the Hearse server at all. That was the
basis for Alexis' earlier comment that it was
"harmless to the server" to just run Hearse after
every game of NetHack.

What I _think_ Hearse does, based on how I see it
behave, is to patch itself with the last time it was
run, after each run, and only pay attention to bones
files that are newer than that time stamp.

In that case, yes, either malice, or in my case
merely shenanigans

  [I tend to reuse "nasty" bones
files until I find a way to "solve" them, as a way
to upgrade my skills, and copying them back and
forth from my other directories where I keep my
backup bones file copies updates a file
"modification" timestamp which seems to be the one
Hearse consults, unfortunately, instead of the
"creation" timestamp], will force a check against
the Hearse server, which then comes back and says:
"already got that one, thanks".

In the more usual case, where a bones file would
perhaps survive on Hearse a lot longer than on
Kent's computer, and the time stamps meanwhile stay
unmodified, that would never occur, and so indeed,
simply discarding a bones file after three times it
was served out, and taking no notes, would suffice,
as the "time stamp" trick would normally prevent it
being re-uploaded from the original user or a
subsequent recipient anyway.

FYI, and Alexis is free to (and I'm sure will)
correct my misunderstandings, since I _don't_ have
the Hearse source code to consult, [or savage me for
re-using bones files, quite possibly, though I take
some precautions to assure the children of those
bones files redropped with more goodies added more
times than once, don't go back into Hearse].

